00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,791
Seventy years of American output growth. Every quarter brings

2
00:00:03,891 --> 00:00:06,790
news nobody expected: a strike, an oil shock, a

3
00:00:06,890 --> 00:00:09,916
good harvest. Take one bad quarter, like this one

4
00:00:10,016 --> 00:00:13,170
in nineteen fifty-eight. How long is it still felt?

5
00:00:14,266 --> 00:00:18,648
Herman Wold answered in nineteen thirty-eight. Every stationary series is

6
00:00:18,748 --> 00:00:21,350
a choir of echoes of its own past surprises.

7
00:00:22,433 --> 00:00:26,904
Think of each surprise as a bell being struck. It rings loudest at the

8
00:00:27,004 --> 00:00:31,084
moment it is struck, and then it fades: a fraction psi one of it

9
00:00:31,184 --> 00:00:35,655
is still heard one period later, psi two after two periods, and so on.

10
00:00:36,733 --> 00:00:39,824
Strike more bells, each with its own force and sign. At

11
00:00:39,924 --> 00:00:42,667
any date, many of them are still ringing at once.

12
00:00:43,366 --> 00:00:48,640
Now add up, date by date, everything that is still sounding. Stack the echoes

13
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:52,827
on top of one another, and the tops trace out a time series.

14
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:57,688
That is the Wold decomposition. The series is its mean plus every

15
00:00:57,788 --> 00:01:02,277
past surprise, epsilon t minus j, weighted by how much of its

16
00:01:02,377 --> 00:01:05,887
echo is left, psi j, with psi zero equal to one.

17
00:01:06,966 --> 00:01:11,157
Which surprises, exactly? Not any shocks we please. Stand at date

18
00:01:11,257 --> 00:01:14,325
t with the whole past in hand, and make the best

19
00:01:14,425 --> 00:01:18,615
linear forecast. The surprise is the gap between what happens and

20
00:01:18,715 --> 00:01:23,236
that forecast: the innovation. By construction no function of the past

21
00:01:23,336 --> 00:01:26,536
can predict it, which is why it deserves the name.

22
00:01:27,633 --> 00:01:30,500
The weights psi j carry the whole personality of

23
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:33,159
a series. Let the echo last longer, and the

24
00:01:33,259 --> 00:01:36,806
same strikes sing a slower, smoother, more persistent tune.

25
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:39,932
Add up all the echoes of one bell and you get

26
00:01:40,032 --> 00:01:43,760
psi of one, the total effect of a surprise. Squared and

27
00:01:43,860 --> 00:01:48,215
times sigma squared, it is the long-run variance of lecture two.

28
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:52,649
Here is a puzzle. Two moving averages of order one:

29
00:01:52,749 --> 00:01:56,099
theta equal to two with unit noise, and theta equal

30
00:01:56,199 --> 00:01:59,413
to one half with noise of standard deviation two.

31
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:03,432
Hold the first echo up to a mirror, reversing time, and it

32
00:02:03,532 --> 00:02:08,855
lands exactly on the second. Autocovariances cannot see the direction of time,

33
00:02:08,955 --> 00:02:12,957
so the twins have the same ones: five, then two, then zero.

34
00:02:13,733 --> 00:02:16,940
Yet only one of them can be recovered from the data.

35
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:20,564
Rebuild the surprises from the past of x, starting from a

36
00:02:20,664 --> 00:02:24,507
